version: "2.0"
description: >-
  Gnomon — the Paradigm 8.0 kernel (docs/specs/gnomon.md). One daemon per user
  owning sessions, identity, the hash-chained log, approvals, the budget fuse
  and the localhost web endpoint; bins gnomon, gnomond and gnomon-web. PR-0
  SCAFFOLD (research 29 §1/§4): the §18 layout with a src/ prefix, placeholder
  bins that print their version, the import fence, the zero-deps check, the
  G-WIN-05 registry skeleton and the M1.0 probe harness. Node >= 24 for Gnomon
  packages only. 8.0 M3 (T-2026-10-21-030): published as @a-company/gnomon with
  the darwin platform packages as optional deps (#gnomon-npm-packaging) and part
  of the root build/test scripts (`npm run build:gnomon` / `npm run test:gnomon`
  still run it alone). Ships to users as the signed Gnomon.app (§18.1).
context:
  - >-
    Package-wide entries live here: #gnomon-kernel, every flow, gate and aspect.
    Component entries with a single home live in a .purpose next to their code
    (src/kernel/supervisor/, src/kernel/local/, src/kernel/desk/,
    src/kernel/git/, src/kernel/loops/, src/kernel/socket/,
    src/kernel/lifecycle/, src/kernel/voice/, src/kernel/web/,
    src/kernel/broker/, src/kernel/identity/, src/kernel/setup/,
    src/kernel/sandbox/, src/kernel/refs/, src/kernel/manifest/,
    src/kernel/log/, src/kernel/budget/, src/kernel/scheduler/,
    src/kernel/runner/, src/kernel/trace/, src/kernel/folders/,
    src/kernel/modhost/, src/cli/, src/adapters/, src/loops/, src/clients/,
    probes/, scripts/); path: values in those files stay relative to
    packages/gnomon.
components:
  gnomon-kernel:
    description: >-
      The kernel package. ZERO runtime deps (G-SEC-01): package.json has no
      `dependencies` key; @a-company/gnomon-contracts is a devDependency that
      tsup bundles. Import fence (G-KRN-02): every import under src/kernel/**
      is node:*, relative, or @a-company/gnomon-contracts, and dist/ holds no
      bare import. src/kernel/ has the thirteen §18 modules (identity, log,
      runner, supervisor, broker, budget, scheduler, manifest, refs, socket,
      web, sandbox, modhost) as empty directories at PR-0. Placeholder bins
      write to stdio directly: the kernel cannot depend on the Paradigm logger
      (zero deps); #kernel-log brings its own logging at M1.2. PR-5 adds
      src/kernel/lifecycle/ — the $daemon-lifecycle core (spec §2.4): home
      resolution (~/.gnomon; GNOMON_HOME is a test seam only, a detached daemon
      gets --home in argv) created 0700 with the mode read back; the endpoint
      path (<home>/gnomon.sock, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR fallback on Linux on sun_path
      overflow, never /tmp, refuse if nothing fits; win32 refuses pending the
      P8-D78 parity phase); the O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile lock lifted from
      warpline lifecycle.ts (live holder refuses, dead holder cleaned by
      rename-and-inode-check and re-raced, empty file = racer mid-write); the
      clean re-exec env (PATH fixed pending root-signed config, HOME/USERPROFILE,
      LANG, LC_*; NODE_OPTIONS and GNOMON_* dropped); the idle-exit monitor
      (holders = open #socket connections until sessions/subscribers exist).
      bins: `gnomond [--foreground] [--home] [--idle-ms]`, `gnomon daemon
      status|stop|--detach` (stop refuses not-implemented pending
      #approval-broker, M1.6). Lifecycle events are emitted to a callback using
      the spec §9.1 kinds daemon.start, daemon.exit{reason}, daemon.lock.refused
      (the kernel log takes them at M1.2); the spec declares no signals.
      SYMBOL CONVENTION (P8-D81, test/symbols-drift.test.ts): every source
      module names the symbol(s) it implements in its header (first 40 lines),
      one `@symbol <id>` per line, using the spec Appendix A id verbatim where
      the spec has one (e.g. `@symbol #kernel-log`, `@symbol $daemon-lifecycle`).
      Every .ts under src/kernel/** must carry a tag; every tag must be
      declared in a packages/gnomon*/.purpose; every declared symbol must be an
      Appendix A node or be listed in test/symbol-allowlist.json with a reason.
      G-WIN-05 MARKER ENFORCEMENT (T-2026-10-17-011): vitest.config.ts excludes
      every test file whose `// @gnomon-platform:` marker omits the running
      process.platform (test/platform-registry.ts excludedTestFiles); each such
      file has a registry row (sibling or n/a reason), and the registry test
      proves per platform that the excluded set equals the rows that omit it,
      and that each row's platforms equal its file's marker.
    cli-map: >-
      src/cli/ by file (2026-10-27 symbol audit). The bins: gnomon.ts (the
      `gnomon` bin: routes each verb to its *-cli.ts module; --version and an
      unknown verb go to placeholder.ts runPlaceholderBin) and gnomond.ts (the
      daemon bin, $daemon-lifecycle, through daemon-cli.ts). readout.ts
      (#gnomon-kernel) is the shared instrument-readout output (aligned
      `label  value` readings, `REFUSED  CODE  reason` with next steps; plain
      text, zero deps). Each verb module is documented by its own entry:
      daemon-cli.ts ($daemon-lifecycle), bare-cli.ts and open-browser.ts
      (#bare-entry, #first-run), up-cli.ts and launch-tty.ts (#gnomon-up,
      #wrapper-channel), dev-cli.ts and dev-home.ts (#dev-launch),
      setup-cli.ts (#first-run), identity-cli.ts (#identity), log-cli.ts and
      replay-cli.ts (#kernel-log), trace-cli.ts (#gnomon-trace), guide-cli.ts
      and guide-topics.ts (#gnomon-guide), rules-cli.ts (#rules-cli),
      mode-cli.ts (#mode-cli), strictness-cli.ts (#strictness-cli),
      local-cli.ts (#local-config-request), loop-cli.ts (#loop-registry),
      session-cli.ts (#session-runner), voice-cli.ts (#gnomon-voice),
      mod-cli.ts (#mod-cli), desk-cli.ts (#offer-router, #mcp-passthrough),
      economy-cli.ts (#turn-economy) and relaunch.ts (#gui-relaunch).
    path: src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, paradigm-8, scaffold]
    components:
      - "#gnomon-probes"
      - "#socket"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#ref-resolver"
      - "#web-endpoint"
      - "#oracle-sandbox"
      - "#manifest-loader"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#session-runner"
      - "#supervisor"
      - "#claude-adapter"
    tests:
      - test/symbols-drift.test.ts
      - test/fence.test.ts
      - test/sec-01.test.ts
      - test/platform-registry.test.ts
      - test/version.test.ts
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
    oracles: [G-KRN-02, G-SEC-01, G-WIN-05, G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02]

flows:
  local-brokered-call:
    description: >-
      $local-brokered-call (TD-2026-10-27-833 slice C1): the model calls an
      offered brokered tool -> #gnomon-local validates the args against the
      offered schema and emits tool_request (deadline 30 s read / 5 min card)
      -> #local-runner parses it (bounded) and hands it to
      #local-tool-broker outside the stdout queue -> resolve -> full-schema
      validation (G-LCL-15: refused args never reach the target) -> read runs,
      mutate/destructive take a #approval-broker permission card showing
      every argument -> #kernel-mcp (kernelMcpAnswer -> #git-verbs) -> shape
      -> one hash-only tool.brokered row -> tool_response -> the child shapes
      it as <tool-data source="kernel" trust="data-not-instructions">. An
      abort or the deadline cancels the call on both sides (an error result,
      a pending card expired, the target never run).
    steps: ['#gnomon-local', '#local-runner', '#local-tool-broker', '#approval-broker', '#kernel-mcp', '#git-verbs']
  local-config-flow:
    description: >-
      $local-config-flow (TD-2026-10-22-769, spec §13.1 "Real homes"): the
      human asks (Settings → Local model, or `gnomon local set`) →
      request.local.probe checks the loopback server first (no card) →
      request.local.config{op:'set'} → #local-config-request validates
      (loopback only) and raises the root `grant` card (local.config) →
      #approval-broker: fresh presence + the root key in ONE helper touch
      (decision.sign, row table local.config, helper 1.9.0) → the effect
      writes local.signed.json (0600, atomic) → the daemon reloads it hot
      (loadLocalConfig → supervisor.setLocalConfig → LocalLane.setConfig,
      probe cache cleared → probeLocal; live sessions keep their spawn-time
      config) → the loop runner's configured() sees it and drops start
      refusals it now serves (recheckStartRefusals; their stack alerts
      clear). Remove: request.local.config{op:'remove'} deletes the file and
      reloads (config.loaded{why:'removed'}), no card.
    steps: ['#local-config-request', '#approval-broker', '#local-runner', '#supervisor', '#loop-runner']
  send-by-word:
    description: >-
      $send-by-word (TD-2026-10-21-514, spec §14.7): the voice client appends
      the spoken send word as its own voice.turn.user (short words by
      adjacency: speakerBasis 'adjacent', accepted only under 1500 ms,
      matching, within 2 s of / pending with the draft speaker) → the kernel
      stamps isWord at receipt (#send-word-matcher, never the text) → the GUI
      (#gui-dictation) sees the word after a draft visible ≥ 1 s, shows the
      1 s grace → request.voice.send.claim{by:'word', turnSeq} (checked,
      consumed atomically, bound to the connection for 5 s) →
      session.send{source:'voice'} gets origin 'voice', sentBy 'word' →
      a record only (#voice-origin: the turn runs like a typed one). Backstop: an unclaimed
      pending word turn stamps voice anyway. Aegis fixes: the origin check
      runs on EVERY human send (stale or wrong-session claims stamp voice;
      under a word+ ceiling an ambient-matched send is a piece, so Enter on
      an ambient draft is stamped voice); a short word passes adjacency with or
      without speakerBasis; a word or pieces attach carries liabilityAck
      (sha256 of the ack text), recorded in the kernel-written start row.
      The guided dialog teaches the word as an ATTENDED TRIAL (spec §14.7
      trial semantics; 2 min, send-word-trial.json 0600): every turn of the
      attached human's voice principal is a take with no speaker check and
      no adjacency, stamped trialTake, forwardable:false, isWord false, never
      a dictation turn or adjacency anchor, and never sent (Aegis M-1: the
      voice principal's direct session.send of a take is refused
      voice-grammar, cause trial-take); a take is captured only while the
      trial is listening, and its row is hash-only plus trialVerdict (Aegis
      M-2: the heard text lives only in kernel memory for status);
      send-word-trial.json is written only from VoiceControl onTrialChange
      with the kernel-clock until and only while listening (Aegis L-2); a
      trial binds to the attached session and ends on any detach (human,
      voice principal, idle) or an attach to another hsid (Aegis L-1); the
      kernel is the ONE take counter
      (status.voice.sendWordTrial, human class only: word, state, take,
      takes, maxTakes 4, correct, variants, last, until) and
      request.voice.sendword{op:'save', dropVariants?} commits the word and
      the variants its mishearings collected (each passing the variant guard,
      #send-word-matcher acceptableVariant), minus the ones the human dropped,
      only after a correct take. request.voice.client mic-toggle /
      mic-key / start{capture} drive the mic, the Mic key and the capture
      kind of the kernel-spawned client (--capture ptt|open|toggle).
    steps: ["#gui-dictation", "#gnomon-voice", "#send-word-matcher", "#supervisor"]
    tags: [gnomon, voice]
    oracles: [G-VOX-20, G-VOX-20b]
  send-by-pieces:
    description: >-
      $send-by-pieces (TD-2026-10-21-514, spec §14.7): the attachment's
      pieces send ceiling (voice.session.start{send:'pieces'}, one fresh
      presence per GUI login, refused at strictness L2 or above) → the GUI
      batches finalized segments by gap/cap (1500 / 8000 ms) and holds while
      the session is busy → request.voice.send.claim{by:'piece', turnSeqs} →
      session.send{source:'voice'} with origin 'voice', sentBy 'piece' and the
      piece guidance note on the per-turn guidance block (#voice-origin).
    steps: ["#gui-dictation", "#gnomon-voice", "#supervisor"]
    tags: [gnomon, voice]
    oracles: [G-VOX-21]
  offer-handoff:
    description: >-
      $offer-handoff (TD-2026-10-20-812, spec §12.9): a reply ends with one
      ```offer block → #offer-router checks budget, gate and declared kind →
      kernel offer.shown (manifest fields) → the GUI slip → ^offer-accept
      (human request.offer.decide) → offer.decided → on open a kernel-templated
      turn as the human → the mod's handoff tool on its gnomon-<mod> server
      (#mcp-passthrough) → offer.kept.
    steps: ["#desk-guidance", "#offer-router", "#mcp-passthrough"]
    tags: [gnomon, desk, mod-playbooks]
  first-run:
    description: >-
      $first-run (spec §2.4 rule 9): unprovisioned Mac → `gnomon` (or `gnomon
      setup`) → #setup-preflight → leftover purge (presence per key) → name →
      #provisioner init (lock, key.list, key.create ×2, root sign 1 of 2 and
      2 of 2 with shown digests, commit identity.json then registry row) →
      summary → #gnomon-up runUp in the same process (TTY launch URL, folder
      hint). Resume: an identity whose registry row is missing is completed
      as its own root-signed step (I10).
    steps: ["#first-run", "#setup-preflight", "#provisioner", "#gnomon-up"]
    tags: [gnomon, setup]

  daemon-lifecycle:
    loops-gate: >-
      TD-2026-10-24-447 (#loops-gate, G-LOOP-29). daemon.ts resolves resolveLoopsEnabled() once
      before wireLoops({enabled}) and emits one daemon.desk loops.disabled when off; the stub
      loops port stays wired as requests.loops.
    windows-home: >-
      BUILT (Windows parity W3; spec §16 Home dir + modes, G-WIN-02). ensureGnomonHome on win32 calls
      platform/owner-only.ts ownerOnlyDir: the win32 helper (platform/win32/helper-client.ts; repo-built,
      sha256-pinned and re-checked before every spawn in a dev kernel, refused unsigned by a release kernel until
      W7) applies the protected owner-only DACL D:P(A;OICI;FA;;;<user SID>) and the home is accepted only from the
      read-back, then becomes a verified root. isOwnerOnly / ownerOnlyFile replace the POSIX mode checks
      (device-key.ts, dev-home.ts so far): a path inside a verified root inherits the ACE and needs no round trip;
      anything else is read back with acl.read. POSIX behaviour is unchanged (0700 / 0600 read back).
    daemon-stop: >-
      PARTLY BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014 B0, TD-2026-10-19-528 F3, spec §3.1 I3).
      Kernel side built: DaemonHandle.requestStop(connId, reason='stop') raises
      (or returns the one pending) signed daemon-stop card through
      broker/daemon-stop.ts DaemonStopCards, only for an authenticated human
      principal on a device connection (every other class, a read token and a
      GUI launch-token connection: FORBIDDEN{matrix}, no card). accept is fresh
      presence (Touch ID; a cached unlock never decides it) and calls
      shutdown(reason) on the next event-loop turn; reject, a declined prompt
      or expiry (2 min, into deny) keep the kernel running. DaemonExitReason
      gains 'stop' (daemon.exit{reason:'stop'}; gnomond exits 0, the
      no-restart class). Concurrent requests share the in-flight raise
      (#raising, Aegis L5): two at once get ONE card. The client path
      (TD-2026-10-19-339) is the human-device verb daemon.stop{reason?}
      (socket/daemon-stop-verb.ts createDaemonStopHandler), on the UNIX
      dispatcher's humanVerbs only (the web dispatcher reports it unavailable);
      connId is the dispatcher's own connection context, never a param (Aegis
      L6); reason is checked against DAEMON_STOP_REASONS (unknown ⇒
      BAD_REQUEST bad-params; upgrade raises the same card with its own
      UPGRADE lines since B1); the
      result is {cardId, state}. `gnomon daemon stop` pins the kernel key
      (kernelPinOf), authenticates as the home's human device (rules-cli
      humanDeviceOf), calls daemon.stop, prints CARD RAISED with "approve it in
      Gnomon with Touch ID" and exits 0 without waiting; no kernel ⇒ REFUSED
      ENGINE not-running; no human device ⇒ REFUSED FORBIDDEN no-human-device.
      B1: DaemonStopReason is the wire's DAEMON_STOP_REASONS (stop, upgrade);
      an accepted upgrade card runs supervisor.drain() and then
      shutdown('upgrade'). Falsifiers in test/daemon-stop.test.ts, test/daemon-stop-verb.test.ts and test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts.
    upgrade-drain: >-
      OLD-KERNEL HALF BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014 B1; TD-2026-10-19-908,
      TD-2026-10-19-528 F3/F4, TD-2026-10-19-814 item 2). daemon.stop{reason:
      'upgrade'} raises the signed daemon-stop card (UPGRADE lines, reason in
      contentHash); a Touch ID accept runs SessionSupervisor.drain({deadlineMs})
      then shutdown('upgrade') (DaemonExitReason 'upgrade', EXIT_REASON_CLASS
      ok: exit 0, no restart). drain(): idempotent; from its first call
      session.open and session.send (Claude and gnomon-local) refuse
      ENGINE{draining}; it waits for opens already past that check, then for
      every Claude session's in-flight turns (turnPermits) to reach a result
      line, up to the deadline (DRAIN_DEADLINE_MS default; no root-signed
      config names one); a session still mid-turn gets the interrupt control
      message, logged session.inject{channel:'drain', deadlineMs,
      deadlineSource}. For EVERY live Claude session: pending cards expire
      why session-drained (F4), session.drained{uuid, reason:'upgrade'} is
      written (closed payload, checked in kernel-log checkPayload), then the
      child stops and session.exited{reason} is written. THE B2 RECORD:
      session.exited.reason is 'drain-clean' (at a boundary: resume normally)
      or 'drain-interrupted' (mid-turn at the deadline: resume IDLE with a
      "turn interrupted by update" note, never continue); DRAIN_EXIT_REASONS
      in supervisor.ts; in-process also DrainReport and status().drained.
      Then ApprovalBroker.expireSessionBound('session-drained') expires every
      other pending card with an hsid; cards bound to no session (install,
      api-token, fallback) stay pending for the new kernel to raise again.
      gnomon-local sessions get no session.drained (they cannot resume until
      B3) and are stopped by shutdown (session.exited{reason:'upgrade'}).
      Falsifiers: test/session-drain.test.ts (darwin), the B1 case of
      test/daemon-stop-verb.test.ts. NEW-KERNEL HALF BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014
      B2): supervisor/resume.ts planResume() reads the kernel-signed rows
      BEFORE the rule-4 sweep: session.drained followed by session.exited
      {drain-clean|drain-interrupted}, with no later session.open or
      permit.reattributed, is resumed; drained with no exit is left to the
      sweep and never resumed. sweepOrphans{resuming} never sweeps a spawn of
      this boot for a resumed hsid. SessionSupervisor.resume() runs the
      ordinary spawn path with --resume <uuid> (same hsid, same uuid): sandboxed
      preflight, law 7, binary pin, layersHash and gitHash must equal the
      drained session's (else session.refused{resume:'layers-changed'|
      'git-changed'}), and BudgetFuse.reattribute() re-attributes the old
      permit.grant's permit (permit.reattributed{permitId, hsid,
      drainedEventId}; no permit.reserve/grant; expired/other-log/not-this-
      session refuse PERMIT_REQUIRED). Nothing is sent to the child; a
      drain-interrupted session also gets session.sandbox{phase:'resume-note',
      note:'turn interrupted by update'}. The new broker holds no unlocks.
      resumeAfterUpgrade() retires every card an earlier kernel left pending
      (ApprovalBroker.retireForeign: approval.expire{why:'cancelled'}); no kind
      is rebuilt from the log (the row lacks the detail its effect needs);
      install cards come back via #mod-host pickup from the staged record.
      A decision against an old card id is NOT_FOUND. The hello feature
      session.resume is advertised (SERVER_FEATURES, already reserved: no
      contracts move); session.send/stop on a resumed session without it are
      refused FORBIDDEN{matrix, needs:'session.resume'}. The daemon exposes
      the run as handle.resume. Falsifiers: test/session-resume.test.ts
      (darwin), test/resume-plan.test.ts (every platform).
      Aegis fixes (T-2026-10-19-014 B1+B2 review): M2 founder carry-over
      ruling — BudgetFuse.reattribute() takes drainedAt + principal; a permit
      live at the drain (drainedAt <= expiresAt) is re-signed under the same
      permitId with a fresh window and permit.reattributed{..., renewedByDrain:
      true, expiresAt}; expired-before-drain refuses; nothing minted
      (test/budget-fuse.test.ts). L1 a kernel-signed session.refused{resume}
      (RESUME_REFUSALS) is terminal in planResume. L2 DaemonStopCards
      refuses a request whose reason differs from the pending card's
      BAD_REQUEST{bad-params, pendingReason, cardId}. L3 principal checked in
      reattribute (assertClaudePermit does not). L4 principalClass carried
      from session.open (resume refuses non-human 'not-human'). L5 a failed
      drain interrupt write logs session.inject{channel:'drain', failed:true}.
      L6 resumeAfterUpgrade retires old cards BEFORE resuming.
      Original plan: A kernel update drains and resumes:
      the old kernel rides `gnomon daemon stop` with reason upgrade (no new
      verb), starts no new turn, drains each session to a turn boundary
      (interrupt after a deadline), writes session.drained{uuid,
      reason:'upgrade'} then session.exited, and exits. The new kernel
      respawns each drained session with --resume <uuid> through the
      respawn path (law-7 preflight, layersHash and permits re-run).
      Cached unlocks and voice arms never carry over; permits are
      re-attributed, never minted; pending cards are raised again. Rule 4
      amended: a drained-and-exited session is not an orphan (G-DMN-04
      covers both cases). Claude sessions first, gnomon-local second; live
      handover is an unprobed idea after Move 2 (spec §22 item 25).
    description: >-
      $daemon-lifecycle (spec §2.4; PR-5 core of M1.9). (1) client activation —
      `gnomon daemon --detach` re-execs gnomond with the clean env and home in
      argv, then waits for its ready line (item 3 below); (2) one per user — home 0700 read back,
      endpoint path chosen (never /tmp), O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile acquired
      (live holder refuses with daemon.lock.refused, dead holder reclaimed;
      Aegis L2: an EPERM holder — another user's pid — is residue and
      reclaimed, and a same-user live holder is `already-running` only if a
      kernel answers hello on OUR socket within 2 s, else `holder-silent`,
      transient, pidfile left alone — a silent holder may be a racer —
      UNLESS its `ps -o lstart=` start witness (TZ=UTC) lies more than a
      2 s tolerance (HOLDER_START_TOLERANCE_MS, a configured bound) after
      the pidfile's startedAt: a recycled pid after a crash or reboot, so
      the pidfile is reclaimed (pid+startedAt+inode re-checked, at most once,
      daemon.lock.reclaimed emitted; T-2026-10-19-017); no witness or an
      unparseable startedAt stays holder-silent);
      (3) #kernel-log opened with the kernel key (M1.4 seam: a supplied or
      ephemeral in-memory software Ed25519 key, custody partial; keystore
      item is M1.3) and the root authority (default unprovisioned), #socket
      listens at 0600 with the hello/auth dispatcher, daemon.start is
      emitted AND logged (daemon.exit logged at shutdown, then the log is
      closed before the lock is released); (4) idle exit —
      no open connections for idleMs (default 10 min) ⇒ daemon.exit{reason:
      'idle'}, socket closed, pidfile and socket released only if still ours;
      SIGTERM/SIGINT take the same path with reason 'signal'. Since M1.4
      slice 2 every log.read{follow} subscription is an idle holder
      ('subscriber') and the hub is closed first at shutdown. Not yet built:
      sessions/timers as holders, mod stop, orphan sweep, the Windows pipe.
      M1.4: the #web-endpoint is started by requestWebLaunch, its WebSocket
      connections are idle holders, and it is stopped (child ended) before
      the socket closes at shutdown. M1.9: (5) home — the daemon takes its
      home only from --home (daemonHomeOf; GNOMON_HOME is a client-side test
      seam it never reads — both gnomond paths, foreground and detaching;
      only `gnomon` client commands honour the seam); (6) orphan sweep at
      start, before the socket listens — every KERNEL-written session.spawned
      with no session.exited/orphaned is recorded session.orphaned{probe,
      action} BEFORE any signal, and its group is signalled only when the
      row's boot session (log/boot.ts) equals this boot AND the live
      leader's start-time witness (process-control.ts: macOS ps lstart,
      Linux /proc stat field 22, read with TZ=UTC before any await at track;
      ps forks — never on a hot path; win32 = parity phase, never signalled)
      equals the recorded one — else probe stale-boot | unattributable,
      action none; orphans stopped in parallel, counted only when gone
      (G-DMN-04); (7) supervised children (DaemonChildren: session,
      local-runner, mod) — intend() writes session.spawned{phase:'intent'}
      before the fork, track() writes {phase:'running', pid, pgid, boot,
      startTime} and refuses a session child that does not lead its own
      group; sessions write session.exited and hold idle ('session'); mods
      never hold, and have no spawn/exit kinds in §9.1, so a crash-orphaned
      mod is not swept (residual); stop = SIGTERM → grace → SIGKILL, then
      group members outliving the leader are SIGKILLed and counted;
      every shutdown (idle included) stops the scheduler, then stops and
      AWAITS every child (mod.stopped{reason}) and the web transport child
      (socket.web.stop{reason:'idle'|'daemon-exit'}) BEFORE daemon.exit
      {reason, liveChildren} is written — the falsifier "daemon exits with a
      live child"; (8) idle holders are connections, subscribers (log.read
      follow AND open GUI WebSockets), sessions and #scheduler timers due
      inside 10 min. Not yet built: the session runner and mod host that
      call children.track (M1.7/M1.8/M1.11), the Windows pipe, job object
      and owner-only DACL read-back (G-WIN-02, parity phase P8-D78).
      T-2026-10-18-030 (1): the clean re-exec passes exactly one node flag,
      --disable-sigusr1 (env.ts DISABLE_SIGUSR1 / reexecArgs), and
      runForeground refuses to start without it in execArgv
      ([sigusr1-enabled]) — a same-user kill -USR1 would otherwise open the
      V8 inspector in the key-holding process; a JS SIGUSR1 listener never
      counts. Both bins use `#!/usr/bin/env -S node --disable-sigusr1`.
      Falsifier: a running gnomond receiving SIGUSR1 opens no inspector and
      keeps running (test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts, control arm proves the
      detector). A SEA must bake the flag into sea-config execArgv.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 1 (descendant sweep): before the first signal a
      session / local-runner stop takes an ASYNC process-table snapshot from
      the kernel (process-control snapshot(): `ps -A -o
      pid=,ppid=,pgid=,lstart=`, TZ=UTC, execFile — never spawnSync on the
      event loop), collects the leader's descendants (ppid walk) and group
      members with their lstart witnesses, SIGTERMs out-of-group ones with
      the group, and after the grace SIGKILLs only pids whose witness still
      matches (a recycled pid is never signalled). session.exited{tree} =
      exited | alive | unverifiable (no snapshot / failed re-check / win32
      ⇒ unverifiable, never folded). Residual: a descendant reparented to
      init before the stop began is unreachable; lstart is 1 s resolution.
      A leader already gone at snapshot time with no group member left ⇒
      unverifiable + treeDetail 'leader-gone-before-stop', never exited.
      stopAll takes ONE snapshot for all its children (T-2026-10-19-011),
      not one `ps` per child; a single stop takes its own.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 3 (readiness handshake, Orca Adopt 3): the
      activator (lifecycle/ready.ts launchDaemon, used by `gnomon daemon
      --detach` and plain gnomond) spawns the kernel with an inherited ready
      pipe (`--ready-fd 3`) and a bounded stderr tail (8 KiB; after an exit
      the tail is judged once stderr ends, waiting at most 250 ms); the kernel
      writes one gnomonReady:v1 {pid, startedAt, socketPath,
      kernelPrincipal} line once it holds the lock and serves, then stops
      writing events to the activator's stderr (EPIPE swallowed). No socket
      polling. A child that exits superseded (lost lock race) or transient
      before ready makes the activator wait for the winner's socket and
      report `attached` (exit 0, no error); no ready line on a live child
      (Windows fallback) = socket probing until the GLOBAL deadline. Probes
      are a 1 s `hello` RPC (not a bare connect) and every probe loop stops
      once the launch settles. Our own live child owning the socket (pidfile
      names it) at the deadline = timeout, never attached. The ready line is
      capped at 4 KiB and must name the child pid AND the expected
      socketPath, else it is rejected (noted in the tail). kernelPrincipal
      from it is an unauthenticated hint, never pinned (only M1.4 mutual auth
      authenticates). gnomond seals the inherited ready fd first thing
      (sealReadyFd: re-open via /proc/self/fd or /dev/fd with O_CLOEXEC,
      close the inheritable one; unsealable => closed) so the identity helper
      and web child cannot forge it. Linux (libuv's stdio pipe is a
      socketpair /proc/self/fd cannot re-open) uses the 'fifo' channel
      instead (T-2026-10-19-011): a private 0600 FIFO in a fresh 0700 mkdtemp
      dir, read end opened by the activator before the spawn, only its path
      passed (`--ready-fifo`), the kernel opens the write end O_CLOEXEC
      (FIFO/owner/mode/inode checked, its parent dir ours and 0700 (Aegis L2), O_NOFOLLOW) and unlinks the name first
      thing, the activator removes the dir at settle; mkfifo runs by
      absolute path (/usr/bin, then /bin, never PATH); a FIFO failure passes
      NO ready channel (never the unsealable fd, Aegis L1): the tail and the
      attached outcome's readyDowngrade record `ready fifo unavailable:
      <code>; probing the socket` and the socket is probed until the global
      deadline. macOS keeps the sealed fd (kqueue never reports the FIFO
      writer's close, losing the no-ready-line fallback). Residual: win32
      handle inheritance; pid + socketPath pin applies on every channel.
      `gnomon daemon status` PINS (T-2026-10-19-016(a)): an unpinned hello
      learns only the CLAIMED serverKeyId; the pin is read from the home
      (root-signed registry kernel row, else keys/kernel-pub.jsonl =
      pinSource home-kernel-pub, same-user custody partial) and a fresh
      pinned hello must verify, then `running:true, serverVerified:true,
      pinSource`. A key the home does not know prints `running:null,
      answering:true, serverVerified:false` with clientApi/serverKeyId only
      under `unverified` (exit 0); a known key whose serverSig fails is
      refused (exit 1). Never running:true on an unverified hello.
      Aegis L4: KernelLog.open runs after the pidfile lock and BEFORE the
      socket is published (the sweep, dispatcher and pre-auth rows need the
      log first; a refused open must not leave a served endpoint). Its
      exclusivity rests on the pidfile lock, which assumes same uid and same
      pid namespace for every contender; a home shared across pid namespaces
      is unsupported (a holder there can look dead and be reclaimed).
      Exit codes are defined ONLY in
      lifecycle/exit-codes.ts: 0 ok, 4 superseded, 64 usage, 70 internal,
      75 transient, 78 config; config/superseded never restart, transient
      and internal restart with backoff. Every LifecycleRefusalReason is
      classified by a Record (a new reason is a compile error until it is).
      Tests: test/daemon-ready.test.ts (POSIX, registry row),
      test/daemon-exit-codes.test.ts (every platform).
    module-map: >-
      src/kernel/lifecycle/ by file (2026-10-27 symbol audit; every file
      carries its @symbol header). $daemon-lifecycle: index.ts (the barrel),
      refusal.ts (LifecycleRefusal and its reasons), home.ts (home resolution,
      0700 read back), endpoint.ts (the socket path or win32 named pipe, never
      /tmp), pidfile.ts (the O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 lock), env.ts (the clean
      re-exec env, reexecArgs and the --disable-sigusr1 guard), idle.ts (IdleMonitor and its holder kinds: connection,
      session, subscriber, timer, loop), process-control.ts (the per-platform
      process-group probe and stop registry), children.ts (DaemonChildren:
      spawn rows, staged stops, the descendant sweep), daemon.ts
      (startDaemon and the DaemonHandle: the wiring of every kernel module),
      ready.ts (client activation: launchDaemon, the ready-line channel and
      the hello probe), exit-codes.ts (the one exit
      code table) and successor.ts (the upgrade successor spawn, used by
      #update-watch). Own entries: build-stamp.ts and update-watch.ts
      (#update-watch), release-pins.ts (#release-pins), app-bundle.ts
      (#app-bundle), app-zip.ts (#gnomon-npm-packaging). The CLI half is
      src/cli/gnomond.ts (the gnomond bin) and src/cli/daemon-cli.ts
      (`gnomond` argument parsing, foreground and detached runs, `gnomon
      daemon status|stop|start`).
    steps:
      - "#gnomon-kernel"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#socket"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#web-endpoint"
    tests:
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
      - test/sigusr1-guard.test.ts
      - test/daemon-children.test.ts
      - test/daemon-children-tree.test.ts
      - test/daemon-ready.test.ts
      - test/daemon-exit-codes.test.ts
      - test/socket-ownership.test.ts
      - test/scheduler-daemon.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02, G-DMN-03, G-DMN-04, G-DMN-05]

gates:
  git-init-gate:
    description: >-
      ^git-init-gate (T-2026-10-23-002; #git-verbs): git.init and
      git.worktree.add/remove run under the session's run mode like an edit.
      manual refuses until a plan is approved; auto-edits runs with an
      approval.auto{modeRef:'mode:auto-edits'} row first (no row, no run: the
      card instead); ask raises a permission card (allow / deny, no Always).
      A force worktree remove always takes its own irreversible card. Loop
      sessions refuse. The folder is re-checked after the decision.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, git, security]
    oracles: [G-GIT-04]
  git-push-gate:
    description: >-
      ^git-push-gate (T-2026-10-23-002; #git-verbs): git.push and repo.create
      are always attended. The card is composed from the kernel's own reading
      (the URL git resolves with the push's own env and flags, the host shown
      plainly, the branch from HEAD), never agent text; a lookalike or
      punycode host is flagged claim-mismatch with no Always; a force push
      (force: true only) is its own irreversible card with a warning line;
      a public repo is irreversible-flagged. git.push honours Always rules
      from the same RuleBook, keyed (repoKey, gnomon_git_push,
      inputHash{verb, host, owner, repo, url}): that exact remote from that
      repo, any branch, never a force push. The remote and branch are re-read
      after the decision (remote-changed refuses). Runs with the human's own
      credentials in a scrubbed env; never tags, never a mirror.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, git, security]
    oracles: [G-GIT-05]
  loop-origin-clamp:
    description: >-
      ^loop-origin-clamp (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.4, LL5; build plan
      step 8; src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts, modelled on the retired
      voice-origin clamp): every loop session opens with origin 'loop'
      (session.open{origin, loopRun}) and only through LocalLane.openLoop
      with a registry run reference. In those sessions standing grants, the
      human's always rules, auto-edits and the read-only passes do not apply
      (loopClampAllows admits only the loop's own trust-card modeRefs, none
      in 8.0; default deny); cards carry detail.origin 'loop', loopId,
      authoredBy and the kernel line "Raised by loop X (authored by Y)"
      (withLoopOrigin, loopClampLine); the session env has no GNOMON_SOCK,
      GNOMON_SESSION_KEY or GNOMON_HSID and judge/summarize have no tools, so
      a loop session has no paradigm_loop write op and no loop.request.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, loops, security]
    oracles: [G-LOOP-05, G-LOOP-06]
  loop-trusted:
    description: >-
      ^loop-trusted (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.1 Supply chain; build plan
      LP2; declared in the 2026-10-27 symbol audit, already enforced by
      #loop-registry and #loop-runner and named in their gates lists): a loop
      runs only at a content hash the human trusted. A repo loop, or any
      changed version of one, is inert until the human decides ONE
      `loop-trust` card (src/kernel/loops/trust.ts): root class (fresh
      presence AND the root key, broker/classes.ts), never `always`, never
      standing (standing-grant.ts NEVER_STANDING_KINDS), never by voice; its
      lines render the whole definition and the diff against the last
      trusted version, and allow writes a root-signed loop.trusted row
      (decision-batch.ts row table `loop-trust`). untrust and delete are
      tightenings with no presence. The runner recomputes the hash
      (loops/hash.ts) before every run and node; a content or config-closure
      change moves the loop to waiting-trust and nothing runs
      (content-changed / closure-changed); resume refuses needs-trust, and a
      `call` node needs its callee trusted at its pinned hash. Cloning a repo
      therefore executes nothing.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, loops, security]
    oracles: [G-LOOP-01, G-LOOP-02, G-LOOP-04, G-LOOP-18]
  mcp-server-enable:
    description: >-
      ^mcp-server-enable (spec §12.10, T-2026-10-23-003; declared in the
      2026-10-27 reconcile, already named in #mcp-servers' gates list): a
      session reaches one of the human's own MCP servers only after the
      human turns it on. request.mcp.enable{id, scope:'all'} or {id,
      scope:'repo', repo} (or the #batch-card form {servers:[…]}) from a
      human gui or device connection raises ONE `grant` card
      (detail.action 'mcp.enable'): root class, hand-only, never standing
      (`grant` is in NEVER_STANDING_KINDS). A server with a problem (a
      law-7 name, a refused env name, a non-https remote, an Anthropic or
      private host, …) cannot be turned on. request.mcp.disable is a
      narrowing: notify-and-proceed, no card (mcp-server-cards.ts). Rows, cards and status carry names, never values.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, mcp, security]
    oracles: [G-MCP-05, G-MCP-07]
  offer-accept:
    description: >-
      ^offer-accept (TD-2026-10-20-812, spec §12.9; Aegis D1): request.offer.decide
      and request.offer.mute are served only for the human on a gui or device
      connection, stamped by connId at dispatch (never an agent, MCP, mod or
      model connection, nor the offer path itself). decide: the offer is live
      (its session running and owned by the asker), the latest for its hsid and
      undecided; exactly one decision per offer, replays refused.
    prizes: []
  setup-token:
    description: >-
      ^setup-token (spec §11.2 rule 11) — the one-shot setup token of the
      #setup-endpoint: 32 bytes, 60 s TTL, single use, carried only in the URL
      fragment #setup=<tok>&k=<spki>[&cwd=…] on the TTY line or the wrapper's
      channel pipe; never argv, env, files or logs. A reuse inside the TTL
      aborts provisioning and journals setup.token-reuse (no token in it); a
      second setup connection is refused.
    prizes: []
  unprovisioned-only:
    description: >-
      ^unprovisioned-only (spec §2.4 rule 9): #first-run provisions only a
      home with NO keys/identity.json. An existing identity is shown
      (createdAt, root keyId) and never adopted or replaced; a malformed one
      is shown, never touched; a dev or symlinked home is refused. The one
      write allowed on a provisioned home is the I10 completion of a missing
      registry row (the saved, already root-signed row; no new signature).
      Declared in the repo-root portal.yaml (gates: unprovisioned-only).
    tags: [gnomon, setup]

  subscription-only:
    description: >-
      ^subscription-only (spec §10 law 7, D35): no kernel-spawned Claude
      process bills API credits — #budget-fuse refuses permit.reserve
      {session.spawn|turn, claude-code} BILLING_REFUSED on any API-billing
      path the law-7 scan finds; the only API path is the per-use root-signed
      api-token card of #approval-broker, injected into exactly one child,
      with the API CREDITS ACTIVE indicator and billing.api.active rows.
      M1.7 slice 1: #session-runner scans every layer the child reads per
      spawn cwd before any permit, #supervisor refuses claude without a
      law-7-scanned claude-code spawn permit and watches the layers
      mid-session (G-SUB-01, G-SUB-05).
    tags: [gnomon, law-7, billing, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    oracles: [G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03, G-SUB-04]

aspects:
  ~ample-internal-logs:
    description: >-
      ~ample-internal-logs (spec §9) — everything the kernel does is an event:v1
      row in #kernel-log with the §9.1 default labels set by the appender
      (audience private; turn.event bodies, session.inject bytes,
      approval.request.tool_input, oracle evidence and voice text sensitive;
      a kind without a default is refused, G-LOG-09), and every read goes
      through the §9.3 redaction matrix (stubs {kind, hsid, at, hash} for
      redacting readers, secret never to a read token, nothing to a peer).
      PR-4 covers the label and read-projection halves; M1.12 slice 3 adds the
      honesty CI (§9.7: G-HON-02, 03, 05, 06, 07; test/honesty.test.ts) and
      the status-number declarations (src/kernel/socket/status-numbers.ts);
      retention/TTL pruning (§9.4) and the remaining §9.6 metrics follow.
    category: observability
    severity: high
    tags: [gnomon, logging, redaction, paradigm-8]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/log/chain.ts
      - src/kernel/log/redaction.ts
    applies-to:
      - "#kernel-log"
